WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.138
This lesson contains two related but different ideas.

2
00:00:04.138 --> 00:00:08.275
Power tells how quickly energy is transferred.

3
00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:12.413
Efficiency tells what fraction of the input becomes useful output.

4
00:00:12.413 --> 00:00:16.551
Learners often merge those ideas because both can sound like performance.

5
00:00:16.551 --> 00:00:20.688
But they are not the same.

6
00:00:20.688 --> 00:00:24.826
A machine can be powerful and inefficient.

7
00:00:24.826 --> 00:00:28.964
It can also be efficient without transferring energy very quickly.

8
00:00:28.964 --> 00:00:33.102
That distinction is the key to the whole lesson.

9
00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:37.239
Keep this takeaway in view: Rate and usefulness must stay separate in your thinking from the start.

10
00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:41.377
Power means rate.

11
00:00:41.377 --> 00:00:45.515
Power answers how fast energy is transferred.

12
00:00:45.515 --> 00:00:49.652
Efficiency means useful fraction.

13
00:00:49.652 --> 00:00:53.790
Efficiency answers how much of the input is useful.

14
00:00:53.790 --> 00:01:00.770
This diagram is helpful because it shows one process can be fast but wasteful, another can be slower but more useful, and a third can be both good or both poor.

15
00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:07.750
That is exactly why power and efficiency both exist.

16
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:14.730
Power describes the rate of energy transfer.

17
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:21.710
Efficiency describes how much of the transferred energy becomes useful output rather than wasted output such as heat or sound.

18
00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:28.690
Keep this takeaway in view: Do not expect one quantity to answer both the rate question and the usefulness question.

19
00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:35.670
Fast process: High power means a high rate of energy transfer.

20
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:42.650
Useful process: High efficiency means a large useful fraction.

21
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:47.451
Imagine a factory line.

22
00:01:47.451 --> 00:01:52.252
One measure is how many items it produces each minute.

23
00:01:52.252 --> 00:01:57.053
That is like power, because it is a rate.

24
00:01:57.053 --> 00:02:01.853
Another measure is how much of the raw material ends up as useful finished product.

25
00:02:01.853 --> 00:02:06.654
That is like efficiency, because it is a useful fraction.

26
00:02:06.654 --> 00:02:11.455
A fast factory is not automatically a low-waste factory, and a low-waste factory is not automatically the fastest.

27
00:02:11.455 --> 00:02:16.256
The analogy makes the lesson logic almost obvious.

28
00:02:16.256 --> 00:02:21.057
Keep this takeaway in view: Power and efficiency belong to different comparison questions.

29
00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:25.858
Per minute output means power.

30
00:02:25.858 --> 00:02:30.658
Rate answers how quickly the transfer or production happens.

31
00:02:30.658 --> 00:02:35.459
Useful yield means efficiency.

32
00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:40.260
Fraction answers how much of the input becomes useful.

33
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.285
Power equals energy divided by time.

34
00:02:45.285 --> 00:02:50.310
That means power is the number of joules transferred each second.

35
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.335
Rearranging gives energy equals power times time.

36
00:02:55.335 --> 00:03:00.360
Efficiency is useful output divided by total input, usually expressed as a percentage.

37
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.385
Notice how the efficiency formula is not about time at all.

38
00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:10.410
That difference is exactly why these relations must be kept separate.

39
00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:15.435
Keep this takeaway in view: The formulas are easier when each one answers a different named question.

40
00:03:15.435 --> 00:03:20.460
Power: How many joules are transferred each second.

41
00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.485
Energy: How much total transfer happens over a chosen time.

42
00:03:25.485 --> 00:03:30.510
P = E / t.

43
00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:35.535
E = P t.

44
00:03:35.535 --> 00:03:40.560
Use watts for power and percent for efficiency when requested.

45
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.491
If the same energy is transferred in less time, the power increases.

46
00:03:44.491 --> 00:03:48.422
If the same power runs for longer, the total transferred energy increases.

47
00:03:48.422 --> 00:03:52.352
If the useful fraction improves while total input stays the same, the efficiency increases.

48
00:03:52.352 --> 00:03:56.283
Those are three different levers.

49
00:03:56.283 --> 00:04:00.214
Time changes rate or total depending on the setup.

50
00:04:00.214 --> 00:04:04.145
Useful output changes efficiency.

51
00:04:04.145 --> 00:04:08.076
Keeping the levers separate prevents formula mixing.

52
00:04:08.076 --> 00:04:12.007
Keep this takeaway in view: Predict the direction of change before you touch the arithmetic.

53
00:04:12.007 --> 00:04:15.938
Same energy, less time means more power.

54
00:04:15.938 --> 00:04:19.868
The transfer is happening faster.

55
00:04:19.868 --> 00:04:23.799
Same power, more time means more energy.

56
00:04:23.799 --> 00:04:27.730
The same rate continues for longer.

57
00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.633
Try three checks.

58
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:33.536
What does power tell you?

59
00:04:33.536 --> 00:04:36.439
The rate of energy transfer.

60
00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:39.342
What happens to transferred energy if the same power acts for longer?

61
00:04:39.342 --> 00:04:42.245
It increases.

62
00:04:42.245 --> 00:04:45.148
And what does efficiency tell you?

63
00:04:45.148 --> 00:04:48.052
The useful fraction of the input.

64
00:04:48.052 --> 00:04:50.955
If those three answers feel distinct, the lesson is ready to move into calculation.

65
00:04:50.955 --> 00:04:53.858
Keep this takeaway in view: Power and efficiency are now separated clearly enough to calculate safely.

66
00:04:53.858 --> 00:04:56.761
Power means rate.

67
00:04:56.761 --> 00:04:59.664
Think joules per second.

68
00:04:59.664 --> 00:05:02.567
Longer time means more total energy.

69
00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:05.470
The same rate keeps adding transfer.

70
00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:10.911
You are ready for the worked example now.

71
00:05:10.911 --> 00:05:16.351
A machine transfers 900 joules in 15 seconds, so power is 900 divided by 15, which is 60 watts.

72
00:05:16.351 --> 00:05:21.792
If that same power continues for 5 seconds, use energy equals power times time.

73
00:05:21.792 --> 00:05:27.233
Sixty times five gives 300 joules.

74
00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:32.673
Then handle efficiency as a separate useful-fraction question.

75
00:05:32.673 --> 00:05:38.114
With 540 joules useful out of 900 joules input, the efficiency is 540 divided by 900, which is 0.60 or 60 percent.

76
00:05:38.114 --> 00:05:43.555
Keep saying what each number means.

77
00:05:43.555 --> 00:05:48.995
Sixty watts is rate.

78
00:05:48.995 --> 00:05:54.436
Sixty percent is usefulness.

79
00:05:54.436 --> 00:05:59.877
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: calculate rate, then total transfer, then usefulness.

80
00:05:59.877 --> 00:06:05.317
Step 1: Calculate the power from energy and time.

81
00:06:05.317 --> 00:06:10.758
Step 2: Use the same power to find energy transferred in the new time.

82
00:06:10.758 --> 00:06:16.199
P = 900 / 15 = 60 W.

83
00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:21.639
E = 60 x 5 = 300 J.

84
00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:27.080
Keep the rate calculation separate from the useful-fraction calculation.
